questions.explanation:
Concept: The zero-sequence component of a three-phase set is

[latex]
V_0=\frac{V_A+V_B+V_C}{3}
[/latex] Case-1: The phase voltages are

[latex]
V_A=V_A\angle\theta_A,\qquad
V_B=V_B\angle\theta_B,\qquad
V_C=V_C\angle\theta_C
[/latex] Therefore, [latex]
V_{0,1}
=
\frac{V_A\angle\theta_A+V_B\angle\theta_B+V_C\angle\theta_C}{3}
[/latex] Case-2: Each phasor is rotated by the same angle [latex]\Delta\theta[/latex], while its magnitude remains unchanged. Hence, [latex]
V_A'=V_A\angle(\theta_A+\Delta\theta)
[/latex] [latex]
V_B'=V_B\angle(\theta_B+\Delta\theta)
[/latex] [latex]
V_C'=V_C\angle(\theta_C+\Delta\theta)
[/latex] The new zero-sequence component is

[latex]
V_{0,2}
=
\frac{V_A'+V_B'+V_C'}{3}
[/latex] Substituting,

[latex]
V_{0,2}
=
\frac{
V_A\angle(\theta_A+\Delta\theta)
+
V_B\angle(\theta_B+\Delta\theta)
+
V_C\angle(\theta_C+\Delta\theta)
}{3}
[/latex] Factoring out [latex]e^{j\Delta\theta}[/latex], [latex]
V_{0,2}
=
e^{j\Delta\theta}
\frac{
V_A\angle\theta_A
+
V_B\angle\theta_B
+
V_C\angle\theta_C
}{3}
[/latex] [latex]
V_{0,2}
=
e^{j\Delta\theta}V_{0,1}
[/latex] Observation: Multiplication by [latex]e^{j\Delta\theta}[/latex] rotates a phasor by [latex]\Delta\theta[/latex] but does not change its magnitude. Therefore, [latex]
|V_{0,2}|=|V_{0,1}|
[/latex]

and

[latex]
\angle V_{0,2}
=
\angle V_{0,1}
+
\Delta\theta
[/latex] Hence, the zero-sequence components in the two cases have the same magnitude but different phase angles.

